A||||6||2birds||Clutch replacement antics of 2birds and his "Ford only" assistant||||1||
Z||000000||2birds||08-15-2000||12:20 AM||||Today I waved the wand over the 86 GT, and said "It's clutch replacement time."  After 35,000 miles, it's slipping pretty regularly after 20-30 miles of interstate driving.<P>Since the whole drivetrain is out and finally apart, I can take pictures of lots of things you don't normally see, and pass on anything of value I find.  Anyone with any requests can e-mail me privately, so I don't fill the forum up with stuff that may already be out there.  <BR>I can't really take the time to go slow like I planned, since my 97 T-bird's intake manifold decided to blow antifreeze out of the front crossover while I was in Jacksonville getting the flywheel turned, and now we are sans vehicle until one gets finished.  At least Ford put out a recall on this one.  Anyone with a plastic intake manifold 4.6 V-8 (Crown Vics, Mustangs, Cougars, Town Cars, and F-150 variants) take heed.||||reg||1
Z||000001||RobertVol||08-15-2000||12:42 AM||||Hey 2Birds <P>Sorry to here about the luck. <P>When you get some details on your progress, let me know and we can update the site. By the way were did you go down here in JAX ? for your work? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||08-15-2000||01:15 AM||||I planned on getting the pics to you once I'm done, and maybe even doing an article for the site.  Tomorrow we'll put it back together, and I'll compose all my notes into something sensible.  I'm going to try to borrow a digital camera from a friend at work, but we may be stuck with the old Kodak Picture Disk from K-Mart.<P>Got the flywheel ground at Dealer's Equipment, off Riverside.  $20 plus tax, and got the press plate, disc, and T/O bearing from them for $113 out the door.   Supposedly the only game in town and I have no problems with them.  Took only 20 minutes as a walk in at 4:30 pm.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||08-17-2000||01:42 AM||||All righty then.<BR>So far, we got it apart Monday, had the flywheel resurfaced, and fought the T-bird blues.  <BR>On Tuesday, I replaced the rear main seal, that was a PITA.  It's a one piece, with a felt lip on the outside, and it didn't want to come out.  My Haynes was worthless here(rare), and the Chilton's listed the two-piece, and the thick and thin one-piece seal as possibles. Since mine was built in June of 86, we assumed it was a thin one piece, and pulled the pan and rear main cap per the directions.  The seal then popped right out, and since the oil pump was off, I decided to throw in a new Melling Hi-volume (20% more) oil pump ($80 for pump and pan gasket, stock oil pump was $35 less).  My 166,000 mile engine was clean inside, and the rear main bearing and journal looked like new.  No loose rod or main bearings, either.  Called it quits after picking up the 'Bird and cut my grass instead...<BR>Wednesday morning was junkyard time.  Jax U-pull it had 2 84-5 4 bangers,, stripped pretty well, and a rebodied 6 with anything useful already gone.  The 4's have front and rear calipers still, but that's about it.  Started again around 1 pm.  Got the oil pump and pan on, replaced the motor and transaxle mounts (front T/A mount was totally rotten, other two not far behind.  Put the flywheel and clutch assy back on, and slid the motor into place.  Took me 15 minutes, about 3 hours less than when I did it with the engine still in the car, and I can still lift my arms above my shoulders this time...<BR>Dragged the cradle assembly back under the car, and used the engine hoist and two jacks to get it into position.  Put in the front cradle bolts, and lifted the rear up (remove the coil and alternator vent pipes first next time).  Connected the coolant lines, exhaust, e-brake cables, and heat shielding, and called it quits around 9 pm.<BR>Tomorrow, it's bleed the rear brakes, and drop it on the ground.  Hook up the fuel and vacuum lines and all wiring, fresh anti-freeze and oil, and I'm on my way.  <BR>I'll post a complete list of obstacles I ran into when I'm done||||reg||1
Z||000004||RobertVol||08-17-2000||11:45 PM||||alright <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||08-18-2000||11:50 PM||||Well, if it could go wrong, it did.<BR>My simple, "bleed the brakes and it's done" idea went out the window first thing yesterday morning, when the left rear caliper bleeder screw twisted apart.  Broke the easy out trying to get it out, and now I have a junk rear caliper.   Got both rears (post recall) from an 85 at Jax U-pull, $12 each, and rebuilt the left one.  By the time I got back from Jacksonville and rebuilt the caliper, it was 6 pm, and time to play family.<P>Today, I bled the brakes, dropped it on theh ground, and hooked up everything on top.  Oil and anti-freeze, and cranked the engine until I got oil pressure.  Fired it up and burnt off all the liquid wrench.  No check engine lights, clutch actually works, and no fuel or coolant leaks.  Test drive showed a very nice clutch engagement now, which I suspect is due to new engine and transaxle mounts all around.<P>New problem, though.  My pedal is barely off the floor, when the clutch starts to engage.  This happened the last time, and bleeding the hydraulics fixed it then so I started to this time, when I discovered my slave cylinder bleeder screw is corroded pretty badly.  I think this is preventing me from getting a good bleed this time, as I'm getting a lot of air (past the threads, maybe) and little fluid in my Mityvac. The dealer says the bleeder screw is only sold with the slave cylinder, and no parts stores have the bleeder either.  Discount Auto can have a new slave cylinder tomorrow ($60)after 1 pm, so I went that route. Rebuild kit is only $15 less.  <P>With my luck, I'll probably twist off the hydraulic line when I remove the old slave cylinder.  I heard that's even harder to replace. <p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 08-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||08-20-2000||11:34 PM||||New slave on, no problems.  Clutch engages real smooth, now.  No shudder on engagement, I think due in part to new engine and transaxle mounts.||||reg||1
